<h2>Why the form of the settlement matters</h2>
<p>Compare the aftermath of default under the two instruments. A settlement recorded only as a contract leaves the innocent party where every contract claimant stands: obliged to establish breach in fresh proceedings — perhaps a fresh arbitration, if the compromise carries its own clause — before any enforcement can begin. A settlement recorded as a Section 30 award skips the adjudication the parties have already made unnecessary: after the Section 34 window closes, the award is enforced under Section 36 in accordance with the Code of Civil Procedure, in the same manner as if it were a decree. Years of difference compress into that distinction.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><strong>Settlement agreement alone</strong><p>Contractual force only; breach must be sued upon afresh; interim protection must be rebuilt; limitation runs anew.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
<div class="col new"><strong>Consent award under Section 30</strong><p>Status of an arbitral award; enforceable as a decree; challenge window narrow and short; execution machinery immediately available on default.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The mechanics, step by step</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Settle during the proceedings.</strong> Section 30 operates on settlements reached &ldquo;during arbitral proceedings&rdquo; — from the reference&rsquo;s commencement until the final award. Parties negotiating on the courthouse steps should conclude before the tribunal is functus officio.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Request the tribunal jointly.</strong> Both parties request that the settlement be recorded as an award on agreed terms. The tribunal may decline — a safeguard against being used to launder collusive or unlawful arrangements — but in genuine commercial compromises it rarely does.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>The award follows Section 31&rsquo;s form.</strong> The consent award is made in accordance with Section 31 — written, signed by the tribunal, dated, stating the seat — and states that it is an arbitral award. Stamp and registration requirements applicable to awards, including those touching immovable property, must be attended to.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Termination and costs.</strong> The proceedings stand terminated; the settlement should deal expressly with costs and the tribunal&rsquo;s fees, or the deposits regime will supply default answers the parties may not intend.</div>
</div>

<h2>Drafting the agreed terms</h2>
<div class="check">
<p><strong>Make obligations executable.</strong> Draft each term as a decree-holder would want it read: amounts, dates, accounts for payment, documents to be executed, properties identified by full description. Vague obligations produce execution disputes that squander the form&rsquo;s advantage.</p>
<p><strong>Build in default consequences.</strong> Acceleration on missed instalments, agreed interest on default, and the revival or waiver of the original claims should be explicit, so execution proceeds on arithmetic rather than interpretation.</p>
<p><strong>Cover all proceedings.</strong> Composite disputes often live in several forums. Record which proceedings are settled, which are to be withdrawn, and who bears their costs — and effect the withdrawals contemporaneously.</p>
<p><strong>Confirm authority.</strong> Board resolutions or written authorisations for the signatories forestall the one Section 34 challenge with realistic prospects: that the consent was given without authority.</p>
</div>
<div class="note"><p>In MSME references under Section 18 of the MSMED Act, and in institutional arbitrations administered by centres such as the DIAC, the same logic applies at the arbitration stage: a settlement reached before the arbitrator is best captured as a consent award, marrying the negotiated outcome to statutory enforceability.</p></div>

<h2>The strategic takeaway</h2>
<p>Section 30 rewards parties who treat settlement as the continuation of enforcement planning rather than its abandonment. Whenever an arbitration settles, the first question should be whether anything remains executory — money to be paid over time, property to be transferred, acts to be done. If yes, the settlement belongs in a consent award. Only where performance is complete and simultaneous — payment against withdrawal, made at the table — does a bare agreement suffice, and even then a recorded termination under Section 32 keeps the file clean.</p>
